# November 22, 2026: a second statewide scatter across Minnesota, region mn, taken by tools/choose.py --thinnest (Minnesota
# had 32 places, the fewest of any state or province). Places spread from the Canadian border at Caribou and the Red River
# country at Argyle to the Otter Tail lakes, the Mesabi and Cuyuna iron ranges, Two Harbors, Little Falls, the prairie
# southwest (Currie, Tyler, New Ulm), the Minnesota River towns, the Mississippi at Mendota and Hastings, and the bluff
# country at Red Wing, Faribault and Homer.
# Facts come from sources opened in this session: Wikipedia read as raw text (wiki_raw.py) and its county Register list
# rows, National Register nomination forms read with page.py (Ramsey Mill 98000872, Larson Mill 73000983, St. Nicholas
# 84001480, Lindbergh House 70000303), MNopedia, SAH Archipedia, the New Ulm Journal of April 15, 2026, and the managing
# bodies' own pages. The Minnesota Historical Society's, Minnesota DNR's, Winona County Historical Society's, Hibbing
# Mine View's, Sheldon Theatre's, Danebod's, Crow Wing County's and the Faribault cathedral's pages are drawn by script, so
# they were read in a headless browser (Playwright) on November 22, 2026, and the hours are what those pages showed that day.
# Brick values come from the sources' words about what each place is built of; '?' where none settled it.
from data_b import N
from data_ma import W
BATCH={'key':'mn_gecb','label':'Minnesota: a second scatter, border to bluff country','added':'2026-11-22 12:28'}
NOM=lambda ref,label: [label,'https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/'+ref+'_text']
MNHS=lambda path,label: ['MNHS: '+label,'https://www.mnhs.org/'+path]
PLACES=[
# ---- The northwest and the Red River country ----
N('st-nicholas-orthodox-church-caribou','St. Nicholas Orthodox Church','Caribou Township','mn','worship','standing',48.98239,-96.45170,'exact','view','drive','','1905',
  'A small wooden church built in 1905 by Ukrainian settlers who had moved south from the settlements round Winnipeg, beside the Roseau River a mile or two below the Canadian line. Most of the parishioners lived in Manitoba; in the 1930s they had to pass through United States customs at Emerson, a trip of seventy to eighty miles each way, to reach it, attendance fell, and regular services ended in the 1940s (SAH Archipedia). It has been used only for occasional events since, and was listed on the National Register in 1984.',
  'A frame church with a square entrance tower under a pyramid roof and two copper-coloured onion domes over the sanctuary and the apse, each topped by a Greek cross of bent metal rod, with a small graveyard of white crosses beside it on open prairie. The Register form of 1984 describes the inside as panelled in unpainted knotty boards, with chairs along the walls, plain wooden benches and an iconostasis covered with icons; interior not checked since. It is not kept open; a regional tourism page says to check access ahead and to respect the cemetery.',
  'Onion domes on the prairie, a church its people reached through customs.',
  accn='Owner not checked. Look from the road; the church is used only for occasional events and no page read gives opening times. The graveyard is part of the site. Kittson County, in the far northwest corner of Minnesota, on local county roads near the Roseau River.',
  links=[W('St. Nicholas Orthodox Church (Minnesota)'),NOM('84001480','National Register nomination form (1984)'),['SAH Archipedia: St. Nicholas Ukrainian Orthodox Church','https://sah-archipedia.org/buildings/MN-01-069-0100']],
  brick='no'),
N('larson-mill-old-mill-state-park','Larson Mill, Old Mill State Park','Argyle','mn','industry','standing',48.366571,-96.567421,'site','public','walk','','1889',
  'A steam-powered gristmill built in 1889 by John Larson, son of Lars Larson, the first miller in the area, on the Middle River on an old beach of glacial Lake Agassiz; the Larsons had homesteaded here in 1882 and run water and wind mills before it. Farmers had flour ground for 50 cents a sack. The state bought the land in 1937; restoration began in 1952, the original 1878 Case steam engine was found, and the mill reopened to the public in 1958 (Register form, Wikipedia).',
  'The mill with its eight-horsepower Case engine of 1878, which the Register form says still drives the original machinery when flour is ground for visitors; Wikipedia says the mill is run as it once was during the park\'s special events. The park also holds a district of eight structures built by the Works Progress Administration in 1937 to 1941. Interior not checked outside events.',
  'An 1878 steam engine that still grinds flour in the Red River country.',
  accn='Old Mill State Park, Minnesota DNR, 33489 240th Avenue Northwest, between Argyle and Newfolden. A state park vehicle permit is needed (self-service QR codes at the unstaffed office, or online). The office is no longer staffed; questions go to Lake Bronson State Park. Park hours from the DNR page, read November 22, 2026.',
  links=[W('Old Mill State Park'),NOM('73000983','National Register nomination form, Larson Mill'),['Minnesota DNR: Old Mill State Park','https://www.dnr.state.mn.us/state_parks/park.html?id=spk00247']],
  th=[48.36190,-96.56988],thn='Old Mill State Park lot on County Highway 40',
  hours='Park daily 8 a.m. to 10 p.m.; the mill runs at special events',brick='?'),
N('phelps-mill-otter-tail','Phelps Mill','Maine Township','mn','industry','standing',46.38046,-95.82109,'exact','public','walk','','1888–1939',
  'A flour mill on the Otter Tail River built in 1888 and 1889 for William E. Thomas, a Fergus Falls flour and feed dealer; its builder, Royal Powers, framed it without blueprints. It opened in October 1889 to make 60 to 75 barrels a day and added buckwheat and rye grinding in 1895. Electric, gasoline and steam power and cheap rail haulage to Minneapolis undid it: it was sold in 1919 and again in 1928 and closed for good in 1939. Otter Tail County bought it in 1965.',
  'The mill on its dam over the river, the centre of a county park, with the general store and an Italianate miller\'s house that make up the Phelps Mill Historic District (Wikipedia). The county has added paved trails, two elevated boardwalks and fishing piers near the mill. Interior not checked.',
  'A mill framed without blueprints, closed in 1939 and kept by the county.',
  accn='Phelps Mill County Park, owned by Otter Tail County since 1965 (the county\'s page), on Phelps Mill Road (County Highway 45) north of Underwood. Park rules and hours from the county page, read November 22, 2026: facilities closed and snow not removed in winter; no parking on the grass.',
  links=[W('Phelps Mill'),['Otter Tail County: Phelps Mill County Park','https://ottertailcounty.gov/location/phelps-mill/']],
  th=[46.38216,-95.82076],thn='Phelps Mill Park lot',
  hours='May to October: daily 6 a.m. to 10 p.m.; November to April grounds open on a limited basis as weather permits',brick='?'),
# ---- The iron ranges, the North Shore and the upper Mississippi ----
N('hull-rust-mahoning-mine-view','Hull–Rust–Mahoning Mine and Mine View','Hibbing','mn','industry','standing',47.44419,-92.92514,'area','open','drive','','1895–',
  'Opened in 1895 and one of the world\'s first mechanised open-pit mines, it is the largest working open-pit iron mine in Minnesota: more than 3 miles long, 2 miles wide and 535 feet deep. From the First World War through the Second it supplied as much as a quarter of the iron ore mined in the United States. It became a National Historic Landmark in 1966. The city\'s old overlook closed because the ground under it was to be mined; the Mine View moved and reopened in 2017, and new buildings were finished in 2025 (Wikipedia).',
  'The working pit seen from the city\'s overlook on the rim, with exhibits on mining and a reclamation pile visitors can climb for the view (the Mine View\'s own page). Active mining, so keep to the viewing area.',
  'A hole three miles long that fed the steel of two world wars.',
  accn='Run by the City of Hibbing at 611 McKinley Street; free. Hours from hibbingmineview.org, read November 22, 2026 (the season\'s opening and closing dates were not given; the gift shop\'s hours differ on the page). The mine itself is an active operation: the pit is not open.',
  links=[W('Hull–Rust–Mahoning Open Pit Iron Mine'),['Hull Rust Mine View (City of Hibbing)','https://hibbingmineview.org/']],
  hours='In season: daily 9 a.m. to 9 p.m. or dusk',brick='no'),
N('milford-mine-memorial-park','Milford Mine Memorial Park','Wolford Township','mn','industry','ruins',46.53472,-93.97083,'area','public','walk','','1917–1924',
  'An underground mine on the Cuyuna Range, worked from 1917 for iron ore rich in manganese and 200 feet deep by 1924. On February 5, 1924 a surface cave-in at its eastern end broke through to mud connected with Foley Lake, the shaft flooded in under twenty minutes, and 41 miners died; seven climbed out. It was Minnesota\'s worst mining disaster. Crow Wing County has made the site a memorial park since 2010, and it was listed on the National Register in 2011.',
  'Interpretive trails past the concrete and stone foundations of the machine shop, the timber shaft and the hoist house, a 450-foot boardwalk over the water, three kiosks and a Memorial Wall to the 41 men (Crow Wing County\'s page and its photographs).',
  'Forty-one miners drowned in twenty minutes; the foundations are still in the woods.',
  accn='Owned by Crow Wing County as a county park (its page), on Milford Lake Park Road, northeast of Crosby, with a parking area, restroom, picnic shelters and a canoe launch. Opening hours not checked; the county page gives none.',
  links=[W('Milford Mine'),['Crow Wing County: Milford Mine Memorial Park','https://www.crowwing.gov/294/Milford-Mine-Memorial-Park']],
  th=[46.53717,-93.97053],thn='Milford Mine Memorial Park lot',
  brick='?'),
N('two-harbors-light-station','Two Harbors Light Station','Two Harbors','mn','light','standing',47.01398,-91.66348,'exact','open','walk','','1891–',
  'Built in 1891 and 1892 and first lit on April 14, 1892, to guide ore boats into Agate Bay, where iron ore from the Mesabi Range was shipped; it is the oldest working lighthouse in Minnesota. Three keepers were normally posted. The Coast Guard replaced the Fresnel lens with an aerobeacon in 1969. The Lake County Historical Society began tours in 1988 under a lease, took ownership in 1999 and has run the light since 2001 (Wikipedia).',
  'A red brick tower 49.6 feet tall with the head keeper\'s house attached, its walls three bricks thick and five where tower and house meet, with the assistant keeper\'s house, fog signal building, oil house, skiff house and garage. The keeper\'s quarters are a bed and breakfast run by the historical society, and the old Fresnel lens came back in 2015 and is shown in its dining room (Wikipedia). The breakwall beside it can be walked.',
  'The oldest working light on the North Shore, with rooms you can sleep in.',
  accn='Lake County Historical Society, at the end of Lighthouse Point Road on Agate Bay. Museum hours not checked: the society\'s own museum page could not be found; the bed and breakfast books through lighthousebb.org.',
  links=[W('Two Harbors Light'),['Two Harbors Lighthouse Bed and Breakfast (Lake County Historical Society)','https://lighthousebb.org/']],
  th=[47.01433,-91.66464],thn='Lighthouse Point Road lot',
  hours='?',brick='yes'),
N('charles-lindbergh-house-little-falls','Charles Lindbergh House','Little Falls','mn','landmark','standing',45.958043,-94.387863,'site','public','walk','','1906–1907',
  'The farm on the Mississippi bluff that Congressman Charles August Lindbergh bought for a dairy farm and where his son, the aviator Charles Lindbergh, grew up. The first house burned in 1905 down to its foundation; Lindbergh\'s business partner Carl Bolander built the present house on the same foundation in 1906 and 1907, which is why the foundation looks too big for it (Register form). The farm is now Charles A. Lindbergh State Park; the younger Lindbergh made his last public address from the porch in 1973.',
  'A one-and-a-half-storey white frame farmhouse on a massive stone foundation, with a red brick chimney through the roof (Register form), two other farm buildings, and stone and log picnic shelter and water tower built by the WPA in the 1930s in the state park. Interior not checked: on November 22, 2026 the Minnesota Historical Society\'s page gave hours only for the trails.',
  'The boyhood farm of the man who flew to Paris, on a foundation too big for its house.',
  accn='House and museum: Minnesota Historical Society, 1620 Lindbergh Drive South, Little Falls; the land round it is Charles A. Lindbergh State Park (Minnesota DNR, vehicle permit for the park). MNHS page read November 22, 2026: trails open daily dawn to dusk, admission free, no house hours given.',
  links=[W('Charles A. Lindbergh State Park'),NOM('70000303','National Register nomination form, Lindbergh House'),MNHS('lindbergh','Charles Lindbergh House')],
  th=[45.95936,-94.38825],thn='Lindbergh Drive lot',
  hours='Trails daily dawn to dusk (house hours not listed)',brick='some'),
# ---- The prairie southwest and the Minnesota River ----
N('currie-turntable-end-o-line','End-O-Line Railroad Park turntable','Currie','mn','rail','standing',44.075187,-95.665668,'exact','open','walk','','1901–',
  'Currie was the end of a 38-mile branch line from Bingham Lake, finished in 1900, and a hand-worked turntable was built here in 1901 by the American Bridge Company of Chicago to turn steam engines round for the run back east. In 1922 the pit was enlarged and a 70-foot turntable, which the park says came from Pipestone, replaced the 56-foot original; it was used until diesels came in the 1950s (MNopedia, the park). In 1972 two girls of a local 4-H club cleared the overgrown site, and the club bought the depot for a dollar, which began the park.',
  'The restored hand-operated turntable, shown working on guided tours, with the 1900 Currie depot, an engine house holding an 1875 Baldwin steam engine, a 1899 section foreman\'s house, an 1890 schoolhouse, an 1873 Presbyterian church, cabooses and the water tower (the park\'s page).',
  'A turntable two teenagers rescued from the weeds.',
  accn='End-O-Line Railroad Park and Museum, a 12-acre Murray County park on the north edge of Currie. Admission charged for the buildings (self-guided or guided); free parking on the west side of the park; the park joins the Casey Jones State Trail. Grounds open daily in daylight. Hours from endoline.com, read November 22, 2026.',
  links=[['MNopedia: Chicago, St. Paul, Minneapolis and Omaha Turntable','https://www.mnhs.org/mnopedia/search/index/structure/chicago-st-paul-minneapolis-and-omaha-turntable'],['End-O-Line Railroad Park: visitor information','https://endoline.com/?p=93'],['End-O-Line Railroad Park: about','https://endoline.com/about-us/']],
  hours='May 23 to September 7, 2026: Wednesday to Sunday 10 a.m. to 5 p.m.; September 18 to October 16: Fridays 10 a.m. to 4:30 p.m.',brick='?'),
N('danebod-tyler','Danebod','Tyler','mn','town','standing',44.26779,-96.13313,'site','open','walk','','1888–1917',
  'The centre of Minnesota\'s oldest Danish immigrant settlement, founded by Danish Evangelical Lutherans of the Grundtvigian school in 1885 at the south edge of Tyler, and named for Queen Thyra Danebod. Its folk school opened in 1888, burned on February 25, 1917, and was rebuilt within nine months; it closed in the 1930s and since 1947 has held summer family camps. Four buildings were listed on the National Register in 1975 (Wikipedia).',
  'Four buildings on a Greek or Latin cross plan: the 1889 Stone Hall of split field rock with crenellations at its entrance, built by the Danish stonemason Kristian Klink; the Cross Church, dedicated on June 16, 1895 and largely built by volunteers; the 1904 clapboard Gym Hall with its twin towers; and the three-storey brick folk school of 1917 with a crenellated corner tower (Wikipedia, the church\'s page). The church is in use; interior not checked.',
  'A Danish folk school and cross church on the prairie.',
  accn='Danebod Lutheran Church and Danebod Folk School, 101 Danebod Court, Tyler; an active congregation, and all are welcome at Sunday worship. Service and office times from danebodlutheran.org, read November 22, 2026. Æbleskiver Days is the fourth weekend of July.',
  links=[W('Danebod'),['Danebod Lutheran Church','https://danebodlutheran.org/']],
  hours='Sunday worship 9:30 a.m.; church office Tuesday to Thursday 9 a.m. to 2 p.m., Friday 9 a.m. to noon',brick='?'),
N('hermann-heights-monument','Hermann Heights Monument','New Ulm','mn','curio','standing',44.30709,-94.47263,'exact','public','walk','','1888–1897',
  'A statue of Arminius, the Cheruscan who beat the Romans in the Teutoburg Forest, known in town as Hermann the German, put up to honour the German ancestry of many Minnesotans. The cornerstone of the setting was laid in 1888, the statue arrived in 1890 and the monument was dedicated in 1897; it was restored in 1998 and 2004 (Wikipedia). In April 2026 a structural inspection found the inside of the monument unsafe, and the city closed it to the public (New Ulm Journal).',
  'A 27-foot statue of sheet copper over iron on a 70-foot iron column with a spiral stair to a dome on ten iron columns and a Kasota stone base, about 102 feet in all (Wikipedia). The stair and viewing platform were closed in 2026; the statue was reported stable. Look from the park.',
  'Hermann the German, 102 feet over the Minnesota River valley.',
  accn='Hermann Heights Park, City of New Ulm, off Center Street; parking at the park. The monument\'s inside was closed to the public in the 2026 season after a structural inspection (New Ulm Journal, April 15, 2026), and signs at the site explain it. Park hours not checked.',
  links=[W('Hermann Heights Monument'),['New Ulm Journal: Hermann Monument to be closed to public (April 15, 2026)','https://www.nujournal.com/news/local-news/2026/04/15/hermann-monument-to-be-closed-to-public/']],
  th=[44.30652,-94.47389],thn='Hermann Heights Park lot off Center Street',
  brick='no'),
N('little-stone-church-ottawa','Methodist Episcopal Church (Little Stone Church)','Ottawa','mn','worship','standing',44.38417,-93.94778,'site','view','drive','','1859–1952',
  'Built in 1859 of locally quarried pink stone for one of the three oldest German Methodist congregations in Minnesota. It closed in 1952; the Le Sueur County Historical Society bought it in 1967 and keeps artifacts in it. It was listed on the National Register in 1982. Ottawa keeps several more limestone buildings of the same years, among them the 1860 township hall and the 1861 Trinity Chapel (Register list).',
  'A small Greek Revival church of pink stone in a village of stone buildings. Interior not checked; no page read gives opening times.',
  'An 1859 stone church in a stone village.',
  accn='Le Sueur County Historical Society. Look from the street; opening times not checked. Ottawa is a small village on the Minnesota River north of St. Peter.',
  links=[W('Methodist Episcopal Church (Ottawa, Minnesota)')],
  brick='no'),
N('cathedral-our-merciful-saviour-faribault','Cathedral of Our Merciful Saviour','Faribault','mn','worship','standing',44.29663,-93.27107,'exact','open','walk','','1862–1869',
  'The oldest cathedral in Minnesota and the first church of the Episcopal Church in the United States designed as a cathedral, by James Renwick Jr. Bishop Henry Benjamin Whipple laid the cornerstone on July 16, 1862 and it was dedicated on June 24, 1869; Whipple is buried beneath the altar. The tower was finished only in 1902, as his memorial, with chimes given by his second wife. Listed on the National Register in 1979 with its guild house (Wikipedia).',
  'Gothic Revival, of native blue limestone from the Fall Creek quarry east of Faribault with red-brown limestone round the windows and doors, on a Latin cross plan with buttresses dividing the nave into seven bays and narrow stained glass windows; the square tower has stone mullions, battlements and crocketed pinnacles (Wikipedia). The cathedral offers free history brochures for visitors and tours by appointment (its tours page).',
  'Bishop Whipple\'s blue limestone cathedral, the first built as one.',
  accn='The cathedral, 515 2nd Avenue NW, Faribault, two blocks from downtown; offices in the Guild House, 101 NW 6th Street. Tours are arranged during office hours by telephone (507-334-7732). Times from faribaultcathedral.com, read November 22, 2026: the tours page says 8:30 a.m. and the contact page 8 a.m.; Sunday service times were not found on the site.',
  links=[W('Cathedral of Our Merciful Saviour'),['The Cathedral of Our Merciful Saviour: tours','https://faribaultcathedral.com/tours'],['The Cathedral of Our Merciful Saviour: contact','https://faribaultcathedral.com/contact']],
  hours='Tours by appointment during office hours, Monday to Thursday morning to noon',brick='no'),
# ---- The Mississippi: Mendota, Hastings, Red Wing, Homer ----
N('sibley-historic-site-mendota','Sibley Historic Site','Mendota','mn','landmark','standing',44.88762,-93.16457,'site','open','walk','','1835–1854',
  'The home of Henry Hastings Sibley, regional manager of the American Fur Company and Minnesota\'s first state governor, built of limestone in 1835 and 1836 by more than a hundred white and Dakota labourers and called the oldest private house in Minnesota. Plans for Minnesota Territory were drawn up in its office. Sibley sold it in 1862 to St. Peter\'s Catholic parish, and Catholic sisters used it as a school and convent from 1867 to 1878 (Wikipedia).',
  'Four limestone buildings on a lawn: the Sibley House, an 1843 fur company cold store, and the 1840 house of trader and hotelier Jean-Baptiste Faribault, with a front of red sandstone, restored from 1934 with Public Works money and opened in 1937; the 1854 Dupuis House, of Milwaukee brick, was a well-known tea house from 1928 to the 1970s and now holds the site offices and the Mendota Mdewakanton Dakota Tribal Community (Wikipedia). Three buildings are shown on seasonal guided tours.',
  'The oldest house in Minnesota, where a territory was planned.',
  accn='Minnesota Historical Society, 1357 Sibley Memorial Highway, Mendota. Admission charged; tickets through the Dakota County Historical Society. Hours from the MNHS page, read November 22, 2026, when it was closed for the season; open on Memorial Day, Juneteenth, July 4 and Labor Day 10 a.m. to 5 p.m. in 2026 (summer days not read).',
  links=[W('Sibley Historic Site'),MNHS('sibley/visit/hours','Sibley Historic Site, hours and admission')],
  hours='Closed for the season September 8 to December 31, 2026; summer guided tours (2026 holidays 10 a.m. to 5 p.m.)',brick='?'),
N('ramsey-mill-ruins-hastings','Ramsey Mill ruins, Old Mill Park','Hastings','mn','industry','ruins',44.72667,-92.84306,'area','public','walk','','1856–1894',
  'A four-storey gristmill built in 1856 and 1857 by Alexander Ramsey, the territorial governor, and Dr. Thomas Foster at the lower falls of the Vermillion River, where a dam gave a fall of about 19 feet. It kept Ramsey\'s name after he sold out in 1877, reached 125 barrels a day in 1894, and burned on December 22, 1894, perhaps by arson; it was never rebuilt. The King Midas Flour Company gave the site to the city in 1925, and it has been a park since.',
  'The Register form of 1998 describes three of the mill\'s four walls of rough Oneota dolomite, about 32 inches thick, still standing in a limestone gorge, the riverside wall partly fallen in 1973, with no roof, floors or machinery; the ruins of the dam, the site of the cooperage, and the hole and retaining wall of the miller\'s house. The walls were deteriorating then. A trail along the steep bluff gives a view of the ruin (Friends of the Mississippi River).',
  'A governor\'s mill burned in 1894, its stone walls still in the gorge.',
  accn='Old Mill Park, a 10-acre City of Hastings park on East 18th Street, with parking at the entrance and a trail system along the Vermillion River. The gorge is steep and the walls were reported deteriorating: keep to the trail. Hours not checked.',
  links=[W('Ramsey Mill and Old Mill Park'),NOM('98000872','National Register nomination form (1998)'),['Friends of the Mississippi River: Old Mill Park','https://fmr.org/old-mill-park']],
  th=[44.72718,-92.84337],thn='Old Mill Park lot on East 18th Street',
  brick='no'),
N('sheldon-theatre-red-wing','Sheldon Theatre','Red Wing','mn','civic','standing',44.56403,-92.53501,'exact','open','walk','','1904–',
  'Built with half the estate that the Red Wing merchant and banker Theodore B. Sheldon left in 1900 for a public purpose, and called the first municipally owned theatre in the United States. It opened on October 10, 1904 with the comic opera The Royal Chef. A task force raised money from 1984 to restore it and it reopened in 1988 (Wikipedia). It still stages dozens of performances a year.',
  'A working theatre of 1904, part of the Red Wing Mall Historic District; inside by ticket to a show. Interior not checked.',
  'The first theatre a town owned, still selling tickets.',
  accn='City-owned theatre at 443 West 3rd Street, Red Wing. Box office hours from sheldontheatre.org, read November 22, 2026; it also opens two hours before any event.',
  links=[W('Sheldon Theatre'),['Sheldon Theatre','https://www.sheldontheatre.org/']],
  hours='Box office September 2026 to June 2027: Wednesday to Friday 1 to 5:30 p.m., Saturday 12:30 to 4 p.m.; performances by ticket',brick='?'),
N('willard-bunnell-house-homer','Willard Bunnell House','Homer','mn','landmark','standing',44.02225,-91.55991,'site','open','walk','','about 1850',
  'Listed on the National Register in 1973 as the first permanent house in Minnesota south of Saint Paul, it was the home of Willard Bunnell (1814–1861), who rose from cabin boy to captain on Great Lakes steamboats, and his brother Lafayette Bunnell (1824–1903) helped open the area. The historical society dates it to about 1850. It was a private house until the Winona County Historical Society bought it in 1954.',
  'A board-and-batten house of unpainted white pine on a limestone foundation in the Carpenter Gothic style, with peaked windows and carved bargeboards, and features more usual on the Ohio and lower Mississippi: a two-storey veranda and a flat-roofed rear wing (Wikipedia, the society). It overlooks Highway 61 and the Mississippi. Shown on self-guided tours and programmes on select summer days.',
  'A steamboat captain\'s Gothic house above the Mississippi.',
  accn='Winona County Historical Society, 36016 Old Homer Road, Homer, about 3 miles east of Winona. Tour days not checked: the society\'s page says select days in summer, and its calendar listed none when read on November 22, 2026.',
  links=[W('Willard Bunnell House'),['Winona County Historical Society: Bunnell House tours and programs','https://winonahistory.org/series/bunnell-house-tours-and-programs/']],
  hours='?',brick='no'),
]
